          "name": "Push and pull nodes between Roam and Obsidian",
          "description": "Inter-graph: Roam/Obsidian",
          "content": "+++ ## Links\n\n* [Roam Project](<https://roamresearch.com/#/app/discourse-graphs/page/rOBMYfhKh>)\n* [Scoping Doc](<https://roamresearch.com/#/app/discourse-graphs/page/iC568HqDZ>)\n* [Open Risks / Questions](<https://roamresearch.com/#/app/discourse-graphs/page/TYHuKimqd>)\n* [Quarterly roadmap prioritizing](<https://roamresearch.com/#/app/discourse-graphs/page/avAAfIthf>) \n\nFollow the playbook for [Obsidian within-lab DG collaboration (Publish & Import)](https://linear.app/discourse-graphs/project/obsidian-within-lab-dg-collaboration-publish-and-import-9e316a1188b0)\n\n+++\n\n+++ ## Goal\n\n* Enable Roam and Obsidian to both act as source and destination for DB-backed Discourse Graph push-pull sync. MVP0 mirrors the existing Obsidian-to-Obsidian reference behavior: a source app shares one selected node to a group, shared persistence stores the node and required dependencies, and a destination app discovers, imports, and refreshes shared nodes from the other app. Assumes [[Project/Sharing group management]] is complete\n* MVP0 / v0 is:\n  * A Roam <-> Supabase <-> Obsidian flow, not a portable-package-first flow.\n  * Single-node-share driven, matching the current Obsidian publish command. Import may still select multiple visible shared nodes.\n  * Manual share/publish grants group access; already-shared source content stays fresh through each source app's existing local-to-remote sync behavior. Pull/import/refresh remains explicit/manual.\n  * Group-targeted. The user chooses a group they belong to when sharing a node, after the separate group-sharing work is available.\n  * Cross-app: Roam can source content for Obsidian, Obsidian can source content for Roam, and both apps can import from the other.\n  * Markdown-first for content transport. [[[[ISS]] - v0 content model]] atJSON is the intended canonical content model, but it is not required for v0.\n  * Built on existing Supabase auth, RLS, group access, content, concept, and file-reference tables where possible.\n\n+++\n\n+++ ## Non-Goals\n\n* MVP0 / v0 is not intended to solve:\n  * Real-time bidirectional merge sync or collaborative conflict resolution.\n  * Full atJSON storage, API negotiation, or app adapter rollout.\n  * A complete portable package implementation.\n  * New local persistence models for either app. Obsidian should keep markdown files, frontmatter, imported assets, and ; Roam should keep pages, blocks, and its existing relation representation.\n  * Multi-node share as a single source operation. MVP0 share mirrors current Obsidian publish behavior: one node per share action.\n  * Full recursive context export. MVP0 should preserve the selected node and required dependencies, not walk the whole reachable graph.\n  * Direct sharing to individual users. MVP0 uses group sharing only.\n  * Automatic remote-to-local polling or background pull into destination apps.\n  * Explicit cross-schema translation, schema-equivalence UI, or automatic merging of incompatible local schemas.\n  * Perfect preservation of every Roam or Obsidian formatting edge case.\n\n+++\n\n+++ ## In-Scope Use Cases\n\n+++ ### UC1: Source user shares one discourse node to a group\n\n> * **Actor:** Roam or Obsidian user\n> * **Trigger:** The user wants collaborators in another Roam graph or Obsidian vault to access one discourse node.\n> * **Happy path**\n>   * 1. The user chooses a single discourse node to share.\n>   * 2. The user chooses one or many target group from the groups they belong to.\n>   * 3. The source app collects the selected node, required node schema, markdown content, origin identity, and any relation or asset dependencies valid to share under the Obsidian reference rules.\n>   * 4. The source app ensures the selected node and required dependency records are persisted through the normal local-to-remote sync contract.\n>   * 5. The source app grants access to the chosen group through shared persistence.\n>   * 6. Later local edits to the shared source node are propagated by the existing local-to-remote sync model; the user does not need to re-share unless the target group changes.\n> * **Version:** MVP0\n\n+++\n\n+++ ### UC2: Destination user discovers cross-app shared nodes\n\n> * **Actor:** Roam or Obsidian user\n> * **Trigger:** The user opens an import surface after being granted access to shared content from the other app.\n> * **Happy path**\n>   * 1. The destination app queries visible shared resources.\n>   * 2. Nodes from the other app appear with source app, source space, title, source-local ID, and modified time.\n>   * 3. Already imported nodes are hidden or marked using stable origin identity.\n> * **Version:** MVP0\n\n+++\n\n+++ ### UC3: Obsidian user pulls Roam-origin nodes into a vault\n\n> * **Actor:** Obsidian user\n> * **Trigger:** The user selects one or more Roam-origin nodes from the import UI.\n> * **Happy path**\n>   * 1. The Obsidian plugin fetches selected content, schemas, relation data, and resolvable asset references.\n>   * 2. The plugin materializes each Roam node as a markdown file under an import folder, with mapped , stable , and frontmatter.\n>   * 3. The plugin imports relations where both endpoints resolve locally, then reports import successes and failures.\n> * **Version:** MVP0\n\n+++\n\n+++ ### UC4: Roam user pulls Obsidian-origin nodes into a graph\n\n> * **Actor:** Roam user\n> * **Trigger:** The user selects one or more Obsidian-origin nodes from the Roam import surface.\n> * **Happy path**\n>   * 1. The Roam extension fetches selected content, schemas, relation data, and resolvable asset references.\n>   * 2. The extension materializes each Obsidian node into Roam using the v0 markdown payload and Roam's markdown import/from-markdown capability.\n>     * And changes to existing nodes are overwritten\n>   * 3. The extension stores stable origin identity so the imported node can be recognized and refreshed later.\n> * **Version:** MVP0\n\n+++\n\n+++ ### UC5: Destination user refreshes an imported cross-app node\n\n> * **Actor:** Roam or Obsidian user\n> * **Trigger:** An imported node has changed upstream and the destination user explicitly asks for the latest shared version.\n> * **Happy path**\n>   * 1. The destination app identifies the source app, source space, and source-local node ID from stored origin identity.\n>   * 2. The destination app fetches the latest shared markdown content and metadata.\n>   * 3. The destination app updates the existing imported node without creating a duplicate and refreshes source timestamps.\n>   * Any changes to the imported node are overwritten.\n> * **Version:** MVP0\n\n+++\n\n+++ ### UC6: Relation between separately shared nodes is preserved\n\n> * **Actor:** Roam or Obsidian user\n> * **Trigger:** The source user shares a discourse node that has a stored discourse relation to another node already shared to the same group.\n> * **Happy path**\n>   * 1. The source app confirms both relation endpoints are shared to the same group.\n>   * 2. The source app writes the relation type schema and relation instance into shared persistence using the same semantic shape Obsidian imports today.\n>   * 3. The destination app maps or creates the local relation type and materializes the relation if both endpoints resolve locally.\n>   * 4. The destination app imports cross-app relation instances as tentative/unaccepted by default.\n>   * 5. Roam stores tentative relation state on the stored/reified relation record\n>   * 6. Tentative Roam relations are shown or discoverable separately from accepted local relations.\n>   * 7. A Roam user can accept or remove a tentative relation. Acceptance promotes it to the normal local relation set.\n> * **Version:** MVP0\n\n+++\n\n+++ ### UC7: Markdown content is later replaced by canonical atJSON\n\n> * **Actor:** Engineer\n> * **Trigger:** MVP0 is working and the team is ready to improve cross-app content fidelity.\n> * **Happy path**\n>   * 1. Roam and Obsidian adapters convert app-native content into a DG-owned atJSON-compatible document.\n>   * 2. Destination adapters render that canonical document into the destination app's native local representation.\n>   * 3. The same canonical content can later render to Roam, Obsidian\n> * **Version:** v1\n\n+++\n\n+++",
